implementationPlan.description:
Write moe-jetbrains/src/test/kotlin/com/moe/toolwindow/BlockerCorpusTest.kt with the Write tool (package com.moe.toolwindow; JUnit 4 + Gson). Model it on DeliveryCorpusTest.kt (landed by VSC-3) and ClientVerdictCorpusTest.kt: loadCorpus, and per-row failure collection.
Header KDoc:
- one client-neutral corpus, three consumers: JetBrains TaskBlockerPresentation here, and VS Code's TaskDetailPanel.ts and media/board.js in VSC-9 (task-06dda5c1, tests/blocker-corpus.test.cjs);
- the CQ-I06b rule: a disagreement is fixed or filed, never given a client-specific expectation;
- only cause, prerequisiteIds and the attention flag are shared; wording differs by client on purpose.
Its error handling: a missing or unparseable /client-blocker.json throws an AssertionError naming the resource. A malformed or throwing row fails THAT row with a message (catch per row, record "threw $e"), so one bad row never hides another.
Two tests:
1. `the corpus is well formed`:
   - version 1, at least 14 rows, ids unique and matching B[0-9]{2};
   - each row has a non-empty string note and a `task` JSON object;
   - the task holds string id and epicId, and uses only the member names step 1 lists (this catches a typo such as blockedOnTaskId);
   - `cause` is one of the three BlockCause names or JSON null; `prerequisiteIds` is an array of JSON strings; `needsHumanReview` is a JSON boolean.
   Collect `"<id>: <problem>"` and finish with one assertEquals("malformed rows", emptyList<String>(), problems).
2. `every row reaches its cause, prerequisites and attention flag`:
   - parse the row's task with MoeJson.parseTask (TASK_CREATED/TASK_UPDATED), as the DoD requires, AND with MoeJson.parseState on {"project":{"id":"proj-corpus","name":"corpus"},"epics":[],"tasks":[task]} (STATE_SNAPSHOT). Both paths read the blocker fields separately (mem:convention-jetbrains-exact-number-parsing). A row whose two parses disagree on any observed value fails, naming both;
   - observed values: TaskBlockerPresentation.cause(task)?.name, TaskBlockerPresentation.prerequisiteIds(task) (list equality, order included) and TaskBlockerPresentation.badges(task).needsHumanReview;
   - collect `"<id>: expected (<cause>, <ids>, <flag>), got (...)"` for every mismatch and finish with one assertEquals("blocker rows that diverge", emptyList<String>(), failures).
Do not edit TaskBlockerPresentationTest.kt, ClientVerdictCorpusTest.kt, DeliveryCorpusTest.kt or any corpus but this one.

Adversarial self-review of this task's diff, the only review step.
- Would the test still pass if:
  - the status gate were dropped (B04, B05, B06, B17 must fail);
  - the dependency check ran before the resource check (B12);
  - a blank resource counted (B13);
  - prerequisiteIds trimmed or deduplicated ids (B10);
  - blank ids counted as a dependency (B11);
  - ids were parsed out of the reason (B08);
  - or attention were tied to BLOCKED (B06, B17)?
- Does every DoD case map to a named row (step 1's coverage map)?
- Is the corpus plain JSON (strings, booleans, arrays, null; no Kotlin names), so VSC-9's TaskDetailPanel.ts and board.js replays read the same bytes unchanged? The VS Code functions already mirror JetBrains: the same guards, order kept, duplicates kept.
- Run `git status --porcelain -- moe-jetbrains` and confirm the task touched only the two new files (plus TaskBlockerPresentation.kt, only for a named bug).
- Peers own every other dirty path: never revert, stage or commit them.
- Report both new files in complete_step.modifiedFiles. Deleting /mnt/d/moe-scratch/vsc4-gate afterwards is fine.
